<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">2010 Haiti earthquake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2010">2010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Goudougoudou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> earthquake was a magnitude 7.0 earthquake that struck Haiti on 12 January 2010, with its epicentre near the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leogane">Léogâne</a>, about 25 kilometres west of the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>. An estimated three million people were affected; estimates of the dead range from 100,000 to about 160,000, while the Haitian government gave 220,000 to 316,000, figures that are disputed. The Presidential Palace, the National Assembly building and the Port-au-Prince Cathedral were damaged or destroyed, and the collapse of the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Stabilization Mission in Haiti killed its chief, Hédi Annabi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2010-haiti-earthquake.jpg" alt="2010 Haiti earthquake" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trocaire from Ireland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_Haiti_National_Palace_-_Port_au_Prince_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, shared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, is seismically active. An earthquake on 7 May 1842 destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sans-souci-palace">Sans-Souci Palace</a>, and one of magnitude 8.0 struck the Dominican Republic on 4 August 1946 and produced a tsunami that killed 1,790 people. Construction standards in Haiti were low and the country had no building codes. Buildings were often raised wherever they could fit, some on slopes with insufficient foundations, and a representative of Catholic Relief Services estimated that about two million Haitians lived as squatters on land they did not own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The earthquake struck at 16:53 local time on 12 January 2010, at a depth of 13 kilometres, on blind thrust faults associated with the Enriquillo-Plantain Garden fault system, and lasted less than 30 seconds. Shaking reached intensity IX on the Modified Mercalli scale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> and its suburbs, and was more severe than for other quakes of similar magnitude because of the shallow depth. The strongest aftershock, of magnitude 5.9, came on 20 January and collapsed seven buildings in Petit-Goâve.</p><p>A localised tsunami, probably from an underwater landslide, hit the beach at Petit Paradis, and at least three people were swept out to sea. Prime Minister Jean-Max Bellerive estimated that 250,000 residences and 30,000 commercial buildings needed to be demolished. The main prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> was destroyed, allowing around 4,000 inmates to escape, and President René Préval escaped unhurt from the National Palace. More than 1,300 schools and 50 health care facilities were destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> was the first country to send aid. Teams from many countries arrived, and a telethon, Hope for Haiti Now, aired on 22 January and raised US$58 million by the next day. The government handed control of the airport to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to ease flight operations, and there was confusion over who was in charge.</p><p>On 22 January the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> noted that the emergency phase was drawing to a close, and the Haitian government called off the search for survivors the next day. Estimates of the dead differ widely. The Haitian government reported 230,000 in mid-February 2010 and, on 12 January 2011, more than 316,000, while a Radio Netherlands investigation suggested about 92,000. Six months after the quake, as much as 98 per cent of the rubble remained uncleared and 1.6 million people lived in camps of tents and tarps.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “2010 Haiti earthquake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
